"Breakfast is the first meal of the day.": "ed5b6fd1190c",
"First, your body breaks the food down. You learned in the lesson about lunch that digestion starts in your mouth and goes on in your stomach and small intestine. Rice, bread, and noodles are mostly starch, and starch is made of long chains of sugar. Your body cuts the chains into single pieces of a sugar called glucose. Glucose is small enough to pass through the wall of the small intestine and into your blood.": "ab51b2b0b320",
"Food is the fuel for your body.": "a700312afeee",
"From the intestine, the blood goes first to your liver. The liver is your sugar bank. When a lot of sugar arrives, a small organ behind your stomach, the pancreas, sends out a signal called insulin. Insulin tells your liver and your muscles to take sugar out of the blood and store it. Hours later, when no food is coming in, the liver lets its stored sugar back out, a little at a time. That is how your blood sugar stays steady between meals, and even while you sleep.": "a32e8cc2c3ea",
"Have you ever felt weak and grumpy just before lunch, and then much better after eating? Food is your fuel. A car burns gasoline, and a phone runs on its battery. You run on rice, noodles, eggs, vegetables, and fruit. But how does a bowl of rice turn into a kick of a ball?": "bd31065a0092",
"Have you ever felt weak and grumpy just before lunch, and then much better after eating? Food is your fuel. A car burns gasoline, and a phone runs on its battery. You run on rice, noodles, eggs, vegetables, and fruit. But how does a bowl of rice turn into a kick of a ball? First, your body breaks the food down. You learned in the lesson about lunch that digestion starts in your mouth and goes on in your stomach and small intestine. Rice, bread, and noodles are mostly starch, and starch is made of long chains of sugar. Your body cuts the chains into single pieces of a sugar called glucose. Glucose is small enough to pass through the wall of the small intestine and into your blood. From the intestine, the blood goes first to your liver. The liver is your sugar bank. When a lot of sugar arrives, a small organ behind your stomach, the pancreas, sends out a signal called insulin. Insulin tells your liver and your muscles to take sugar out of the blood and store it. Hours later, when no food is coming in, the liver lets its stored sugar back out, a little at a time. That is how your blood sugar stays steady between meals, and even while you sleep. The blood carries the glucose to every cell. Inside the cell, glucose meets the oxygen you breathed in, and the cell “burns” the sugar slowly, without a flame, to get energy. What is left over is water and carbon dioxide, which you breathe out. So eating and breathing are two halves of one job. Your muscles use this energy to move. Your brain is a big user, too: an adult's brain takes about a fifth of the energy the body uses at rest. And your body uses energy all night just to stay warm and keep your heart beating. Not every food gives up its sugar at the same speed. In a sweet drink, the sugar is already dissolved, so it rushes into your blood. Your blood sugar shoots up, a lot of insulin follows, and soon the sugar is put away and you feel hungry again. A meal with rice, an egg, and vegetables is broken down slowly, so the sugar arrives little by little, and the energy lasts all morning. Your body needs more than sugar, of course. It needs protein to build muscles, calcium for bones, and many other things, and no single food has them all. That is why Taiwan's Health Promotion Administration draws a plate with six food groups: whole grains, protein foods, vegetables, fruit, dairy, and nuts and seeds. It even has a rhyme, and one line says that you should have as much rice as vegetables. Now use the plate tool below the model: which groups did you eat today?": "f6d8fb18c771",
